{
  "slug": "pronunciacion",
  "number": 1,
  "part": 1,
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "The sounds of Spanish",
  "tagline": "Five pure vowels, the letters that surprise English speakers, and where the stress goes.",
  "goal": "Pronounce the five Spanish vowels, read the tricky letters (j, g, h, ll, ñ, rr, c, z), and know which syllable to stress just by looking at a word.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "vocales",
      "title": "Five vowels, always the same",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "English vowels change all the time (cat, car, care). Spanish has only **five vowel sounds**, short and clean, and each letter always sounds the same. No gliding: *no* is «no», not «noh-oo»."
        },
        {
          "type": "sounds",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "a",
              "ipa": "a",
              "hint": "like the a in «father», but shorter",
              "examples": [
                "casa",
                "mamá",
                "banana"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "e",
              "ipa": "e",
              "hint": "like the e in «bet»",
              "examples": [
                "mesa",
                "leche",
                "tres"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "i",
              "ipa": "i",
              "hint": "like the ee in «see», short",
              "examples": [
                "sí",
                "libro",
                "vino"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "o",
              "ipa": "o",
              "hint": "like the o in «more», without the w at the end",
              "examples": [
                "no",
                "foto",
                "como"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "u",
              "ipa": "u",
              "hint": "like the oo in «food», short",
              "examples": [
                "tú",
                "uno",
                "luna"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "Every vowel is pronounced, even at the end of a word: *noche* has two syllables, «no-che», and *gente* is «gen-te»."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "consonantes",
      "title": "The letters that surprise you",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Letter",
            "Sound",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**h**",
              "always silent",
              "*hola, hotel, ahora*"
            ],
            [
              "**j**, and **g** before e / i",
              "a raspy h, from the throat",
              "*jamón, gente, Gijón*"
            ],
            [
              "**g** before a / o / u",
              "hard g, like «go»",
              "*gato, amigo, guerra* (gu: silent u)"
            ],
            [
              "**ll** and **y**",
              "like the y in «yes» in most countries",
              "*llamar, playa, yo*"
            ],
            [
              "**ñ**",
              "like the ny in «canyon»",
              "*niño, mañana, España*"
            ],
            [
              "**rr**, and **r** at the start",
              "a rolled r (several taps)",
              "*perro, rojo, Roma*"
            ],
            [
              "**r** between vowels",
              "one quick tap, like the tt in «butter» (US)",
              "*pero, cara, para*"
            ],
            [
              "**v** and **b**",
              "the same sound, a soft b",
              "*vino, bien, uva*"
            ],
            [
              "**z**, and **c** before e / i",
              "s in Latin America, th in most of Spain",
              "*zapato, cine, cena*"
            ],
            [
              "**qu**",
              "k (the u is silent)",
              "*queso, quince, aquí*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "pero / perro",
          "text": "One tap or a roll changes the word: *pero* = but, *perro* = dog. *caro* = expensive, *carro* = car."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "acento",
      "title": "Where the stress goes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Spanish spelling tells you which syllable to stress. Two simple rules, and a written accent (´) whenever a word breaks them:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Word ends in…",
            "Stress falls on…",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "a vowel, **n** or **s**",
              "the second-to-last syllable",
              "*ca{sa}, ha{blan}, za{pa}tos*"
            ],
            [
              "any other consonant",
              "the last syllable",
              "*ha{blar}, ciu{dad}, espa{ñol}*"
            ],
            [
              "a written accent",
              "the accented syllable, always",
              "*ca{fé}, {lá}piz, can{ción}, {mú}sica*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "Accents also separate words that sound the same: *tú* (you) / *tu* (your), *sí* (yes) / *si* (if), *él* (he) / *el* (the). Questions always carry them: *qué, dónde, cómo*."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
